vmsnotify @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (@ARCH@)
======================================

(Adapted from vmsotel's kit/readme.vms, issinoho/vmsotel, MIT, same author.)

A notification agent: it reads this system's operator log (OPERATOR.LOG)
and security audit journal and sends what matters -- login failures,
break-in attempts, operator messages, whatever the rules in
VMSNOTIFY.CONF select -- to a Gotify server, an Apprise API server, ntfy
or a webhook that you run yourself. It connects out to them; nothing
listens on this system. It runs as its own account, never as SYSTEM,
with NETMBX, TMPMBX and READALL, and no more.

Requires: OpenVMS x86-64 (tested on E9.2-4), Integrity (tested on
V8.4-2L3) or Alpha (tested on V8.4-2L2), VSI TCP/IP Services, and VSI's
SSL3 kit. There is no VAX kit.

Setting up (as SYSTEM)
----------------------
1. Create the agent's account, set the protections, and create the
   configuration, once:

     $ @VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[COM]VMSNOTIFY$CONFIGURE

   It asks for the account (default VMSNOTIFY) and, for a new account,
   its UIC: choose a group of its own, above MAXSYSGROUP (e.g. [360,1]).
   The account cannot log in by any route; the agent still runs as it.
   Its authorized privileges are NETMBX, TMPMBX and READALL. It changes
   only an account it created.

2. Edit VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]VMSNOTIFY.CONF (made from
   VMSNOTIFY.CONF_EXAMPLE, which explains every setting): one
   [endpoint NAME] section per server, and the rules. Keep each token
   in a file of its own in [CONF] and name it, rather than writing it in
   the configuration:

     [endpoint home]
     type = gotify
     url = https://gotify.example.org
     token_file = VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]GOTIFY.TOKEN

   [CONF], [DATA] and [LOG] belong to the account and nobody else can
   read them. A file you add to [CONF] must be the account's, with no
   group or world access:

     $ SET FILE/OWNER=VMSNOTIFY VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]GOTIFY.TOKEN
     $ SET FILE/PROTECTION=(S:RWED,O:RWED,G,W) VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]GOTIFY.TOKEN

   For https the server's certificate is always verified, against the
   system's authorities or the file named by cafile (put that in [CONF]
   too); there is no setting to skip it.

   Check what it would send, without contacting anything, and send one
   test notice to every endpoint:

     $ VMSNOTIFY := $VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[BIN]VMSNOTIFY_TLS.EXE
     $ VMSNOTIFY "VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]VMSNOTIFY.CONF" "--dry-run" "--cycles" "2"
     $ VMSNOTIFY "VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]VMSNOTIFY.CONF" "--test"

   (Quoted, because DCL lowercases what is not. SYSTEM can read the
   account's files, so this shows the configuration as the agent will
   read it; neither writes the read position.)

3. Start it:

     $ @VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[COM]VMSNOTIFY_START

   and stop it, letting it send what it has queued:

     $ @VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[COM]VMSNOTIFY_STOP

   Both need IMPERSONATE and WORLD (SYSTEM has them).

4. At boot and shutdown, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM (after
   TCP/IP) and SYS$MANAGER:SYSHUTDWN.COM:

     $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSNOTIFY$STARTUP.COM
     $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSNOTIFY$SHUTDOWN.COM

   VMSNOTIFY$STARTUP starts the agent if VMSNOTIFY$CONFIGURE was told to
   (SYS$MANAGER:VMSNOTIFY$CONFIG.COM).

Files
-----
  VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[BIN]   VMSNOTIFY_TLS.EXE (used when present: it also
                         speaks plain HTTP), VMSNOTIFY.EXE,
                         VMSNOTIFY_LAUNCH.EXE (starts the agent as its
                         account)
  VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[COM]   VMSNOTIFY_START, _STOP, _RUN, VMSNOTIFY$CONFIGURE
  VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[CONF]  VMSNOTIFY.CONF, VMSNOTIFY.CONF_EXAMPLE, the
                         token files and certificates
  VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[DATA]  VMSNOTIFY.POS (how far each log has been read,
                         kept across restarts)
  VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[LOG]   VMSNOTIFY.LOG (the agent's, one line per event),
                         VMSNOTIFY_RUN.LOG (DCL's, five versions kept)
  VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[DOC]   this file, OPERATIONS.MD, LICENSE.
  SYS$MANAGER:VMSNOTIFY$CONFIG.COM   the account, and whether to start
                         at boot

PRODUCT REMOVE VMSNOTIFY stops the agent and deassigns VMSNOTIFY$ROOT and
VMSNOTIFY$USER. VMSNOTIFY.CONF, the tokens, the read position, the logs,
the site settings and the account are kept, so a reinstall carries on
where it left off.

When it does not start, VMSNOTIFY$ROOT:[LOG]VMSNOTIFY_RUN.LOG says why;
when it does not send, VMSNOTIFY.LOG does. The source and the full
documentation are at https://github.com/issinoho/vmsnotify.

